Looking at this problem, I need to find all integers from 1 to 999,999 where every value generated by recursively applying either digit-sum or digit-product (any choice, any depth) remains odd.

## Key Observations

**Parity conditions for a number N with k digits:**
- **Product of digits is odd** ⟺ all digits are odd (any even digit makes the product even)
- **Sum of digits is odd** ⟺ the count of odd digits is odd. If all digits are odd, this means k must be odd

**No even-digit starting code works:** if N is even, its last digit is even, so P(N) is even — a generated value is even. ✗

**No even-length number works:** if k is even and all digits are odd, then S(N) = sum of k odd digits = even — a generated value is even. ✗

So candidates must have **all odd digits** and **odd length** k ∈ {1, 3, 5} (k=6 is even, excluded).

**Recursion terminates:** both operations strictly decrease multi-digit numbers, reaching single-digit fixed points. A single-digit d is "good" (all descendants odd) ⟺ d is odd, i.e., d ∈ {1,3,5,7,9}.

**No 2-digit or 4-digit number is ever good** (even length → sum is even), which constrains intermediate values.

## Defining "Good"

Good(N) ⟺ N is odd, Good(S(N)), and Good(P(N)).

Since 2-digit numbers are never good, any intermediate value that is 2-digit immediately fails.

## k = 1 (single digit)

Good ⟺ odd. Count: **5** (namely {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}).

## k = 3 (three digits, all from {1,3,5,7,9})

S(N) = a+b+c ∈ [3, 27]. For Good(S(N)): S(N) must be a good 1-digit number (2-digit values 11–27 are never good). So S(N) ∈ {3, 5, 7, 9}.

Enumerating all (a,b,c) with odd digits summing to {3,5,7,9}:

| Sum | Combinations | Count | Product P(N) | P good? |
|-----|-------------|-------|-------------|---------|
| 3 | (1,1,1) | 1 | 1 | ✓ |
| 5 | (1,1,3) perms | 3 | 3 | ✓ |
| 7 | (1,1,5), (1,3,3) perms | 3+3 | 5, 9 | ✓ |
| 9 | (1,1,7) perms | 3 | 7 | ✓ |
| 9 | (1,3,5) perms | 6 | 15 | ✗ (2-digit, not good) |
| 9 | (3,3,3) | 1 | 27 | ✗ (2-digit, not good) |

Good 3-digit count: 1 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = **13**

## k = 5 (five digits, all from {1,3,5,7,9})

S(N) = a+b+c+d+e ∈ [5, 45]. For Good(S(N)): only 1-digit good values are reachable (max 45 is 2-digit, never good). So S(N) ∈ {5, 7, 9}.

Enumerating all (a,b,c,d,e) with odd digits summing to {5,7,9}:

| Sum | Combinations | Count | Product P(N) | P good? |
|-----|-------------|-------|-------------|---------|
| 5 | (1,1,1,1,1) | 1 | 1 | ✓ |
| 7 | (3,1,1,1,1) perms | 5 | 3 | ✓ |
| 9 | (5,1,1,1,1) perms | 5 | 5 | ✓ |
| 9 | (3,3,1,1,1) perms | C(5,2)=10 | 9 | ✓ |

All products are single-digit odd → all good. Good 5-digit count: 1 + 5 + 5 + 10 = **21**

## Verification of excluded cases

- **(1,3,5) → P=15**: S(15)=6 (even) → 15 generates an even number. ✗
- **(3,3,3) → P=27**: P(27)=14 (even) → 27 generates an even number. ✗
- Any 3/5-digit number with S(N) ≥ 11: S(N) is 2-digit, never good. ✗
- Any number with an even digit: P(N) is even. ✗
- Any even-length number: S(N) is even. ✗

Spot-checks: N=111 → S=3, P=1, both fixed odd points ✓. N=117 → S=9, P=7, both fixed odd ✓. N=33111 → S=9, P=9, fixed odd ✓. N=135 → P=15 → S(15)=6 even ✗.

## Final Count

| Digits | Good count |
|--------|-----------|
| 1 | 5 |
| 2 | 0 |
| 3 | 13 |
| 4 | 0 |
| 5 | 21 |
| 6 | 0 |

**Total = 5 + 13 + 21 = 39**

